.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause
.TH DCERPC 1 2026-10-14 libdcerpc "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dcerpc \- send DCE/RPC requests written in YAML to an SMB2 server
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dcerpc
.RB [ \-j | \-\-json ]
.RB [ \-c | \-\-coder
.IR file.so ]
.I smb2-url
.RI [ request.yaml ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dcerpc
connects to the
.B IPC$
share of an SMB2 server, binds to a DCE/RPC service over a named pipe,
and sends one or more requests to it.
Each request is written in YAML.
.B dcerpc
encodes it as NDR, sends it, and prints the decoded reply as YAML,
or as JSON with
.BR \-j .
.PP
The requests are read from
.IR request.yaml ,
or from standard input when no file is given, which makes a shell
here-document a convenient way to write them.
.PP
Each request starts with a line naming the procedure, followed by its
indented
.B [in]
parameters:
.PP
.RS
.nf
NetrShareEnum: Request
  ServerName: \e\efs1
  ...
.fi
.RE
.PP
Blank lines and lines starting with
.B #
are ignored.
Several requests can be given in one input, separated by a line that
contains only
.BR \-\-\- .
They are sent one at a time, in order, on the same connection, and each
reply is printed before the next request is sent.
.PP
Each reply is printed after a line containing
.B YAML:
and a
.B \-\-\-
line, or after a line containing
.BR JSON: .
The reply holds the
.B [out]
parameters and the return value as
.BR Status .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-json
Print the replies as JSON instead of YAML.
The requests are always YAML.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-coder " \fIfile.so\fR"
Load an additional interface from a coder that the libdcerpc IDL
compiler generated and that was built as a shared object, for example
.IR axpy_coder.so .
The service name is the file name without the
.I _coder.so
suffix, and the object must export the symbols
.IB name _interface
and
.IB name _procs\fR.
A name without a
.B /
is loaded from the current directory.
The option can be given more than once.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print a short usage message and exit with status 0.
.SH URL
.RS
.nf
smb://[\fIdomain\fR;][\fIuser\fR@]\fIhost\fR[:\fIport\fR]/IPC$/\fIservice\fR[?\fIarguments\fR]
.fi
.RE
.PP
The share must be
.BR IPC$ ,
and the path names the service.
The services that are built in are:
.TP
.B srvsvc
Server service (shares, sessions, open files, server information).
.TP
.B wkssvc
Workstation service.
.TP
.B winreg
Remote registry.
.TP
.B lsarpc
Local Security Authority (SID and name lookups, policy information).
.TP
.B samr
Security Account Manager (domains, users and their groups and aliases).
.TP
.B dssetup
Directory Services Setup (the server's role and domain), reached through
the
.B lsarpc
pipe.
.TP
.B netlogon
Netlogon (secure channel setup).
.TP
.B epmapper
Endpoint mapper.
.PP
Services loaded with
.B \-c
are added to this list.
.PP
The URL arguments are those of libsmb2, for example
.B sec=ntlmssp
or
.B sec=krb5
to choose the authentication, and
.B seal
to encrypt the connection.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
With NTLMSSP the user is taken from the URL, or is the name of the
local user when the URL does not name one.
The password is read from the file named by the
.B NTLM_USER_FILE
environment variable.
Each line of that file has the form
.PP
.RS
.IB domain : user : password
.RE
.PP
Only the lines that name the user are used; user names are compared
without regard to case.
Of those,
.B dcerpc
uses the password from the first line whose first field is the domain
given in the URL or the host part of the server name.
A line with an empty first field matches any domain and server, and is
used when no other line matches.
An empty line ends the file.
.PP
With Kerberos
.RB ( sec=krb5 )
the credentials come from the Kerberos credential cache instead.
.SH PLACEHOLDERS
A request can use values from the replies to the requests before it,
for example the handle returned by an open call.
Before a request is sent, every
.BI @ name @
in it is replaced by the value of the field
.I name
in the previous reply, and every
.BI @ name :\- n @
by the value of the field
.I name
in the reply
.I n
requests back.
When a reply has more than one field with that name, the first one is
used.
.PP
.RS
.nf
OpenPolicy2: Request
  SystemName: \e\efs1
  ObjectAttributes:
    Length: 24
    Attributes: 0
  DesiredAccess: POLICY_VIEW_LOCAL_INFORMATION
\-\-\-
Close: Request
  PolicyHandle:
    ContextHandleAttributes: 0
    UUID: @UUID@
.fi
.RE
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NTLM_USER_FILE
The file with the NTLM passwords, see
.BR AUTHENTICATION .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
All requests were sent and a reply was received for each of them.
A reply can still carry an error in its
.B Status
field.
.TP
.B 1
The command line is not valid.
.TP
.B 9
The URL, the service name or a request is not valid, or a placeholder
could not be resolved.
.TP
.B 10
The connection, the bind or a call failed.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the shares on the server
.B fs1
with
.B NetrShareEnum
at level 1, which returns the name, type and comment of every share.
.PP
First create a file with the password, readable only by you:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ cat ~/.ntlm
EXAMPLE:alice:Secret.Passw0rd
$ chmod 600 ~/.ntlm
$ export NTLM_USER_FILE=~/.ntlm
.fi
.RE
.PP
Then send the request as a here-document:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ dcerpc 'smb://EXAMPLE;alice@fs1/IPC$/srvsvc?sec=ntlmssp' <<EOF
NetrShareEnum: Request
  ServerName: \e\efs1
  InfoStruct:
    Level: SHARE_INFO_1
    ShareInfo:
  PreferedMaximumLength: 0xffffffff
  ResumeHandle: 0
EOF
YAML:
\-\-\-
NetrShareEnum: Response
  InfoStruct:
    Level: SHARE_INFO_1
    ShareInfo:
      Level1:
        EntriesRead: 4
        Buffer:
          \- NetName: ADMIN$
            Type: STYPE_DISKTREE | STYPE_SPECIAL
            Remark: Remote Admin
          \- NetName: C$
            Type: STYPE_DISKTREE | STYPE_SPECIAL
            Remark: Default share
          \- NetName: IPC$
            Type: STYPE_IPC | STYPE_SPECIAL
            Remark: Remote IPC
          \- NetName: projects
            Type: STYPE_DISKTREE
            Remark: Project files
  TotalEntries: 4
  ResumeHandle: 0
  Status: 0
.fi
.RE
.PP
The
.B EOF
is not quoted, so the shell expands
.B $
in the request.
Quote it as
.B <<'EOF'
when a value contains a
.BR $ .
.PP
The same request with the reply printed as JSON:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ dcerpc \-j 'smb://EXAMPLE;alice@fs1/IPC$/srvsvc?sec=ntlmssp' <<EOF
\&...
EOF
.fi
.RE
.SH REQUEST EXAMPLES
The
.I libdcerpc/dcerpc\-examples
directory of the libsmb2 source has example requests for the srvsvc,
wkssvc, winreg, lsarpc, samr, dssetup, netlogon and epmapper procedures.
The files are named
.IB interface \- procedure .yaml\fR,
for example
.IR srvsvc\-NetrShareGetInfo.yaml ,
and can be passed to
.B dcerpc
directly:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ dcerpc 'smb://alice@fs1/IPC$/srvsvc' srvsvc\-NetrShareGetInfo.yaml
.fi
.RE
.PP
They are also available online:
.PP
.RS
.nf
https://github.com/sahlberg/libsmb2/tree/master/libdcerpc/dcerpc\-examples
.fi
.RE
.PP
The names and types of the fields come from the IDL file of the
interface, in
.IR libdcerpc/ interface / interface .idl .
.SH NOTES
libdcerpc is experimental, and the YAML that describes a request can
change between releases.
.SH SEE ALSO
.UR https://github.com/sahlberg/libsmb2
.UE
